# S12.1 final-review delta: Git hardlinks, rollback durability and ORIG_HEAD

Date: 2026-09-30
Scope: source-only correction before merge/freeze/deploy
Deployment attempts consumed: 0

## Findings

1. The root Git metadata walk rejected symlinks and special files but did not
   reject regular files with multiple hard links. A root Git write could
   therefore mutate an external alias.
2. Repository data was `syncfs`-flushed while the recovery barrier was active,
   but ownership/mode restoration occurred while leaving the barrier. The
   rollback-complete marker could therefore follow metadata changes that had
   not received a final filesystem flush.
3. Backup/rollback captured HEAD, release branches and the managed freeze tag,
   but not the Git pseudo-ref `ORIG_HEAD`. A failed attempt could consequently
   leave a rollback-side `ORIG_HEAD` mutation behind.
4. The Git metadata hardlink gate did not cover regular files in the canonical
   worktrees. An existing external hardlink could remain writable after the
   repository parent and root permissions were locked.
5. Release input bundle digests were checked by pathname and the same paths
   were reopened for verification and import before the shared repository
   parent was locked. A writable ancestor allowed rename/replacement between
   those operations.
6. Link-count validation alone could not detect a process that opened an
   external hardlink and then deleted that alias. Its retained descriptor or
   mapping still addressed the canonical inode while exposing no in-tree path.
7. Ref and `ORIG_HEAD` restoration did not remove reflog entries appended by
   release synchronization and recovery checkout. Expressions such as
   `HEAD@{1}` could therefore retain failed-attempt history after GREEN
   rollback.
8. After the repository barrier was released, a completed `chatops` write
   could occur before a later acceptance failure and then be overwritten by
   rollback.
9. A valid attached branch name beginning with `-` was ambiguous to the
   bounded `git branch -f` restoration command.
10. A partial synchronization failure occurred before the complete post-sync
    drift record existed and released the active barriers before rollback.
11. Moving the complete rollback body under newly acquired repository barriers
    allowed barrier contention to delay restoration of the temporary public
    nginx callback route.
12. An interruption after repository restoration but before durable rollback
    completion caused recovery to compare the already-restored state against
    the earlier post-sync state and made the controller's own rollback
    non-resumable.
13. The restore phase journal remained durable after a restore error, but the
    context manager still removed the physical Git barriers. Recovery then
    correctly refused to resume `RESTORE_STARTED`, leaving no safe path to
    complete the interrupted restore.
14. A successful repository restore released its writer exclusion before the
    rollback-complete marker was written. An ordinary repository writer could
    therefore alter the restored checkout in that finalization interval while
    the controller still emitted GREEN rollback evidence.
15. A clean canonical checkout could contain an ignored local file at a path
    newly tracked by the reviewed release. Git checkout permits replacement of
    ignored files, while the repository backup intentionally contains only
    versioned state, so rollback could not recover the displaced local bytes.
16. A clean checkout could carry noncanonical Git index flags such as
    `assume-unchanged` or `skip-worktree`. Checkout/merge and rollback rebuild
    index entries and could silently lose those pre-attempt flags while the
    commit, tree and porcelain status still appeared restored.
17. Barrier teardown recursively assigned the recorded top-level owner to all
    nested worktree entries. A root-owned ignored file below a chatops-owned
    checkout could therefore become chatops-readable; redundant `chown` calls
    could also discard ownership-sensitive inode metadata.
18. After release synchronization, a path removed from the tracked tree could
    become ignored. A later chatops writer could create local state at that
    path; release-state validation ignored it and rollback checkout would
    overwrite it with the backed-up tracked version.
19. The new ownership precondition accepted only the original checkout-root
    owner. Crash recovery could encounter a durably journaled intermediate
    after root locking or after reversing a Git-directory exchange and reject
    that controller-created state before the idempotent unwind could run.

## Resolution

- Require every regular entry in the complete quarantined Git metadata tree to
  have link count one before any root Git operation.
- Flush both canonical repository filesystems again after barrier removal and
  ownership/mode restoration, before clearing the barrier journal or writing
  rollback completion evidence.
- Record `ORIG_HEAD`, include a present value in the authenticated bundle,
  validate its object, and restore and verify either the exact value or exact
  absence. The restore-index schema advances to V5 so an older record cannot
  be interpreted as an intentional absent value.
- After locking the shared repository parent and rejecting retained handles,
  require link count one for every regular file throughout both worktrees
  before locking their roots or running root Git.
- Open both release input bundles once with `O_NOFOLLOW`, validate the opened
  inode and digest, then copy only from those pinned descriptors into the
  root-owned stage after repository-parent locking. Verify, inspect and import
  only those protected, digest-checked copies.
- Build device/inode identities for the complete protected trees and compare
  every retained cwd/fd plus writable or shared `/proc/*/maps` entry against
  them. Deleted external aliases therefore remain detectable.
- Snapshot the complete `.git/logs` tree (including exact absence) inside the
  durable root-private backup, bind its file bytes and modes to a SHA-256 tree
  digest, and replace/verify the reflog tree after all rollback Git operations.
  Advance the incompatible backup contract to schema V6.
- Persist the exact clean post-sync repository states in the root-private
  attempt marker. Rollback now reacquires the repository barrier and validates
  those states before changing either runtime files or repositories; drift is
  fail-closed and never overwritten.
- Reject option-like attached branch names during backup and again during
  restore validation.
- Roll back partial synchronization and immutable-stage failures through the
  already-held repository barriers, then finalize only after those barriers
  are durably restored.
- Restore, validate and reload the backed-up nginx state before attempting to
  reacquire repository barriers on later failures.
- Journal `RESTORE_STARTED` before the first sync/restore mutation and advance
  to `REPOSITORIES_RESTORED` while barriers remain held. Recovery resumes a
  started restore only with the physical barriers still installed, while a
  restored phase performs cleanup/finalization without replaying Git changes.
- Preserve the physical repository and parent barriers when any restore body
  fails, including exceptions during resumed recovery. The exception remains
  visible and a subsequent recovery can safely continue from the durable
  `RESTORE_STARTED` phase.
- Bind `REPOSITORIES_RESTORED` to the complete restored repository states.
  Finalization reacquires the barriers, revalidates those states and writes the
  rollback-complete marker before releasing writer exclusion. If barrier
  cleanup is interrupted after that marker, recovery removes only the
  lingering barrier and never replays repository restoration.
- Enumerate ignored worktree paths and the exact release trees from the sealed
  offline fetch stage using bounded NUL records. Before backup and again before
  the attempt marker, reject every exact or ancestor/descendant collision with
  `S12_1_IGNORED_RELEASE_COLLISION_RED`; no path or content enters output or
  retained evidence.
- Require the entire tracked index to have canonical `H` entries before backup
  capture and during backup race validation. Any assume-unchanged,
  skip-worktree, unresolved or otherwise noncanonical entry fails closed with
  `S12_1_BACKUP_REPOSITORY_INDEX_RED`, so rollback never has an unrecorded
  index flag to reproduce.
- Before any parent or repository barrier mutation, and again after the parent
  is locked, require every non-Git worktree entry to match the recorded root
  owner/group and every Git entry to match the recorded Git owner/group.
  Heterogeneous ownership is therefore rejected before deployment. Teardown
  excludes `.git` from the worktree traversal and performs `chown` only when
  an entry actually differs, preserving ownership-sensitive metadata on all
  unchanged entries.
- Store only domain-separated SHA-256 hashes of the exact pre-state tracked
  paths and their ancestors in authenticated, root-private backup blobs. The
  backup contract advances to V7. Under the rollback barrier and before any
  stage removal or repository restore, hash every current ignored path and its
  ancestors and reject any collision with
  `S12_1_ROLLBACK_IGNORED_COLLISION_RED`. No repository path or content is
  retained in this additional evidence.
- Keep initial deployment strict, but let journal-backed rollback/recovery
  accept only the finite original, owner-restricted and root-locked top-level
  states produced by the barrier state machine. Nested entries remain limited
  to the recorded owner or root-owned controller transition state. This makes
  the journal resumable without admitting arbitrary ownership drift.

The correction does not alter product boundaries, credential handling, the
single-attempt contract or deployment scope.
